WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.668
-Our next guest is
a best-selling author

00:00:02.668 --> 00:00:04.370
and Oscar-winning screenwriter
whose books include

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.672
"A Prayer For Owen Meany,"
"The World According to Garp,"

00:00:06.672 --> 00:00:08.641
"The Hotel New Hampshire,"
and "Cider House Rules."

00:00:08.641 --> 00:00:09.742
His 15th novel,

00:00:09.742 --> 00:00:11.577 line:0%
"The Last Chairlift,"
is out now.

00:00:11.577 --> 00:00:15.415 line:0%
Please welcome to the show
John Irving, everybody.

00:00:15.415 --> 00:00:18.184
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:33.932
Welcome to the show, John.
I'm so honored to have you here.

00:00:33.932 --> 00:00:36.936
-Thank you. Thank you.
-Now, this is your 15th book.

00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:40.072
You're a prolific writer.
This is 900 pages.

00:00:40.072 --> 00:00:43.243
This is the longest one.
Did you know when you set out?

00:00:43.243 --> 00:00:47.179
-I sincerely hope
it's the longest one.

00:00:47.179 --> 00:00:50.649
And I'm almost certain
that's the longest one.

00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:52.585
-Yeah, you're not
going to beat this record?

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:55.387
-No. There's only short trains
in the station.

00:00:55.387 --> 00:00:57.390
That's it.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.192
-When you had the idea,

00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:02.161
did you know this was
going to be the long one?

00:01:02.161 --> 00:01:05.732
-I knew it would be long.

00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:11.637
I didn't know it would be my
longest or longer --

00:01:11.637 --> 00:01:15.274
forgive me --
than "Bleak House,"

00:01:15.274 --> 00:01:17.477
almost as long as
"David Copperfield."

00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:19.345
I didn't know
it would be that long.

00:01:19.345 --> 00:01:26.152
But when you follow the lives of

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:31.056
the main characters'
extended family --

00:01:31.056 --> 00:01:34.761
his cousin, her girlfriend,
his mother,

00:01:34.761 --> 00:01:38.498
her girlfriend,
and the stepfather,

00:01:38.498 --> 00:01:41.667
who transitions to female --

00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:44.237
you have a lot of lives
to follow.

00:01:44.237 --> 00:01:47.172
-Yeah.

00:01:47.172 --> 00:01:50.777
-And it's my intention

00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:58.151
to take each of those
characters to an ending.

00:01:58.151 --> 00:02:00.119
-You -- I think a lot of
writers begin with

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:01.754
an idea of how
a book should start.

00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:03.989
I've heard you say
you like to begin knowing

00:02:03.989 --> 00:02:05.324
what the ending is going to be.

00:02:05.324 --> 00:02:08.761
Have you always found
that reverse engineering to be

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:11.431
the right approach
for you, creatively?

00:02:11.431 --> 00:02:14.333
-Always, yes, but I needed to
write four books

00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:18.170
before I realized
it wasn't going to change.

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:20.005
I needed to realize

00:02:20.005 --> 00:02:23.709
that there wasn't something
really wrong with me.

00:02:23.709 --> 00:02:26.712
It's that I see endings first.

00:02:26.712 --> 00:02:31.050
I don't have the confidence
to begin a novel

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:36.489
unless I'm writing toward
an ending that I have foreseen,

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:40.326
that is, in my mind,
predetermined.

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:44.664
And that's --
It's just always been that way.

00:02:44.664 --> 00:02:47.133
-Well, certainly as a reader,
I appreciate when

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:49.669
that moment, sometimes of dread,
when you realize an author

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:52.338
might not know
where this was going.

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:54.707
And so I like when you --
the confidence when

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:56.542
you're reading,
you know, again, an author

00:02:56.542 --> 00:02:57.977
that you have
a relationship with.

00:02:57.977 --> 00:02:59.912
Being in safe hands
is a nice feeling.

00:02:59.912 --> 00:03:02.614
You sort of mentioned
a lot of the characters,

00:03:02.614 --> 00:03:03.816
and maybe people
picked up on it,

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:07.086
but the protagonist
is a straight white male

00:03:07.086 --> 00:03:10.156
surrounded by LGBTQ characters.

00:03:10.156 --> 00:03:14.226
Is this -- Was it a plan of
yours to write about

00:03:14.226 --> 00:03:16.896
his perspective of that world
or to make him sort of

00:03:16.896 --> 00:03:19.566
the outsider
amongst that group?

00:03:19.566 --> 00:03:26.372
-It was certainly my intention
to make him the odd guy out,

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:29.842
to make him the queer
member of the family,

00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:34.680
queer in the sense of strange
and not up to speed.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:40.453
And he is the slowest member of
his extended family.

00:03:40.453 --> 00:03:44.023
He's learning from
all the rest of these people.

00:03:44.023 --> 00:03:48.527
And I don't think it will be
a surprise to anyone

00:03:48.527 --> 00:03:54.000
that he's also,
as the only straight guy,

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.602
the most badly behaved sexually.

00:03:56.602 --> 00:04:01.673
-Yeah. You've been writing about
sexual and gender politics

00:04:01.673 --> 00:04:03.075
for a very long time.

00:04:03.075 --> 00:04:05.344
I feel like,
you know, perhaps the --

00:04:05.344 --> 00:04:07.079
you know,
the take on those topics

00:04:07.079 --> 00:04:08.681
from your readership
has changed.

00:04:08.681 --> 00:04:09.982
Has it made it easier
to write about,

00:04:09.982 --> 00:04:11.150
has it made it harder
to write about,

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:13.419
more satisfying to write about?

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:17.923
-It's made it depressing
that it hasn't gone away.

00:04:17.923 --> 00:04:20.559
-The issues?
-Yeah.

00:04:20.559 --> 00:04:25.964
I remember, when I was starting
"The World According to Garp,"

00:04:25.964 --> 00:04:31.704
that I was even naive enough
to imagine that a novel

00:04:31.704 --> 00:04:36.308
about these extremes of
sexual intolerance,

00:04:36.308 --> 00:04:41.748
these extremes of sexual hatred,
I honestly thought

00:04:41.748 --> 00:04:45.584
the novel was
in danger of being passé,

00:04:45.584 --> 00:04:49.221
or out of date,
before I published it.

00:04:49.221 --> 00:04:52.091
-You -- Another book of yours,
"Cider House Rules,"

00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:54.093
which took place in the '40s
and was sort of a book

00:04:54.093 --> 00:04:59.031
about what would happen if
abortion was illegal,

00:04:59.031 --> 00:05:02.134
is it also shocking to you
that we haven't --

00:05:02.134 --> 00:05:03.836
or it seems as though maybe
we moved past it

00:05:03.836 --> 00:05:06.572
and now maybe we're moving back?

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:10.676
-Well, we sure are moving back.

00:05:10.676 --> 00:05:14.714
I remember, at the time,
some of my friends

00:05:14.714 --> 00:05:23.322
thought it was quaint that I was
writing an "historical novel"

00:05:23.322 --> 00:05:29.194
about that time when abortion
was unsafe and illegal.

00:05:29.194 --> 00:05:31.964
And what they meant was,

00:05:31.964 --> 00:05:37.470
"Now that we have Roe v. Wade,
now that it's secure."

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:45.945
Well, I wrote that novel
intentionally as a warning,

00:05:45.945 --> 00:05:51.751
because there were
already the voices in --

00:05:51.751 --> 00:05:56.589
The anti-abortion zealots
were already there,

00:05:56.589 --> 00:06:01.694
and this was my way of saying,

00:06:01.694 --> 00:06:03.495
"Everything that happens
in this story

00:06:03.495 --> 00:06:05.631
can only happen at a time

00:06:05.631 --> 00:06:10.636
when this procedure
is unsafe and illegal.

00:06:10.636 --> 00:06:15.107
Do you really
want to go back there?"

00:06:15.107 --> 00:06:21.981
And it doesn't make me
feel prescient.

00:06:21.981 --> 00:06:23.182
I'm not prescient.

00:06:23.182 --> 00:06:27.720
I'm really bad
at guessing the future.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:33.692
But I've also been really bad at
underestimating the pushback

00:06:33.692 --> 00:06:37.730
against everything progressive

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:41.299
in women's rights
and LGBTQ rights.

00:06:41.299 --> 00:06:46.072
I underestimate that
there's going to be a pushback.

00:06:46.072 --> 00:06:47.239
-Yeah.
Progress is not permanent.

00:06:47.239 --> 00:06:49.308
-And that the pushback
doesn't go away.

00:06:49.308 --> 00:06:52.945
-Yeah. This is really special to
have you here tonight.

00:06:52.945 --> 00:06:56.549
I grew up reading your books.
My mom is your biggest fan.

00:06:56.549 --> 00:06:58.083
She came to the show tonight.

00:06:58.083 --> 00:07:00.486
She went in your dressing room
beforehand with me.

00:07:00.486 --> 00:07:02.255
Overall, how do you think
she behaved?

00:07:02.255 --> 00:07:05.191
[ Laughter ]

00:07:05.191 --> 00:07:09.195
-I thought her behavior
was exemplary.

00:07:12.097 --> 00:07:15.401
I was very happy to meet her.

00:07:15.401 --> 00:07:18.204
-Well, you have
not sacrificed your role

00:07:18.204 --> 00:07:20.639
as her favorite living author.

00:07:20.639 --> 00:07:22.875
You clinched it.
You clinched it right there.

00:07:22.875 --> 00:07:24.076
John Irving, everybody.

00:07:24.076 --> 00:07:26.112
"The Last Chairlift"
is available now

00:07:26.112 --> 00:07:27.380
wherever you buy your books,

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:29.381
and please support local
and independent bookstores.

00:07:29.381 --> 00:07:32.218 line:0%
We'll be right back with music
from Rina Sawayama.

